{"causes":["The rise of children's literature in the late 19th and early 20th centuries created a fertile ground for stories like Anne's.","Lucy Maud Montgomery's personal experiences as a woman in a patriarchal society influenced the themes of independence and resilience in the novel.","The cultural backdrop of early 1900s Canada, which emphasized community and family, shaped the narrative's focus on belonging and identity."],"effects":[""Anne of Green Gables" has influenced countless adaptations in film, television, and theatre, expanding its reach beyond literature.","The novel sparked a renewed interest in Canadian cultural identity, leading to a greater recognition of Canadian authors and stories.","Anne Shirley's character has become a symbol of imagination and individuality, inspiring generations to embrace their creativity and uniqueness."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"lucy-maud-montgomery","sourceName":"Lucy Maud Montgomery","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"anne-of-green-gables","targetName":"Anne of Green Gables","context":"Lucy Maud Montgomery created "Anne of Green Gables" in 1908, establishing a foundational work in Canadian literature."},{"sourceSlug":"prince-edward-island","sourceName":"Prince Edward Island","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"anne-of-green-gables","targetName":"Anne of Green Gables","context":"The events of "Anne of Green Gables" occur in Prince Edward Island, which serves as the backdrop for Anne's adventures."},{"sourceSlug":"canadian-literature","sourceName":"Canadian Literature","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"anne-of-green-gables","targetName":"Anne of Green Gables","context":""Anne of Green Gables" has had a lasting influence on Canadian literature, inspiring subsequent generations of writers."},{"sourceSlug":"children's-literature","sourceName":"Children's Literature","verb":"FRAMES","targetSlug":"anne-of-green-gables","targetName":"Anne of Green Gables","context":"The novel is framed within the broader context of children's literature, showcasing the shift towards more complex themes in stories for young readers."},{"sourceSlug":"cultural-icon","sourceName":"Cultural Icon","verb":"DEFINES","targetSlug":"anne-of-green-gables","targetName":"Anne of Green Gables","context":"The character of Anne Shirley has come to define the archetype of the imaginative young girl in literature and pop culture."}],"places":[{"name":"Charlottetown, Canada","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Avonlea, Canada","role":"Setting"},{"name":"Toronto, Canada","role":"Publication site"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Anne of Green Gables","type":"Reference article","slug":"anne-of-green-gables"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q565911","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anne_of_Green_Gables"],"tags":["Canada","literary work","Americas","Novels & Prose Fiction","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Anne of Green Gables contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Anne of Green Gables is a novel by Lucy Maud Montgomery (1874–1942), published on 13 June 1908 by L. C. Page & Company of Boston — one of the most beloved and internationally successful Canadian novels ever written, with over 50 million copies sold in translations into at least 36 languages, and the founding text of a series of eight Anne novels that follow Anne Shirley from orphaned childhood to married maturity. The novel follows the red-haired, imaginative, fiercely intelligent Anne Shirley — mistakenly sent to the middle-aged siblings Matthew and Marilla Cuthbert of Green Gables farm in Prince Edward Island in the expectation of a boy to help with farm work — who wins over her reluctant adoptive family and community through her irrepressible personality, her verbal brilliance, and her emotional warmth, eventually winning a scholarship to the Queen's Academy in Charlottetown.

Anne of Green Gables was written from Montgomery's own experience of Prince Edward Island life — the rural landscape of PEI (which Montgomery described as 'the most beautiful place in the world'), the small-town social culture of Avonlea, and the aspiration toward education and self-improvement that characterised the lives of women in late 19th-century rural Canada. The novel's extraordinary success immediately upon publication — it went through six printings in its first year and made Montgomery an internationally known author — established it as a classic of children's literature and of Canadian literature, and transformed Prince Edward Island into a major literary tourism destination. Mark Twain praised it as 'the sweetest creation of child life yet written', and it was enormously popular in Japan, where the character of Anne became a cultural icon.

Anne of Green Gables is a landmark in the representation of female intelligence and ambition in children's literature — Anne's passionate commitment to education, her verbal creativity, her refusal to accept the limitations placed on women, and her emotional courage established the template for the independent-minded, intellectually ambitious girl protagonist that influenced subsequent children's literature.","subjects":["Canadian Literature","Modern Era","Lucy Maud Montgomery","Children's Literature","Female Protagonists","20th Century","Literary Tourism","Girls' Fiction"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":""Anne of Green Gables" has played a crucial role in shaping Canadian cultural identity and has influenced global perceptions of children's literature, leading to adaptations that have reached millions.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
